e parts of the of  the country  that we have set out yeah we have a  situation at the moment in which there  are you know  local authorities in direct  contravention of the advice coming from  the department for education  telling their head teachers this morning  if you want to close the schools that's  absolutely fine with us it's a rather  chaotic situation  we have the neu nes uwt all of them  saying  schools are not safe not just for pupils  to go back into  but for teachers if frontline  professionals in the education system  are telling you that they are not  confident going back into school on what  basis  do you say that schools are safe well on  the basis of the public health advice  which i think is incredibly important as  the  uh uh as the core question  that we're facing is how do we deal with  this pandemic  and the public health advice is a is  absolutely critical to that  so that's what people should be  listening to the advice of the advice is  that the data suggests that young people  are  less affected by the most severe effects  of of the coronavirus  that doesn't mean it's safe for teachers  yes there's also  it's also clear that the proportion of  teachers who  catch coronavirus is no higher than the  rest of the population  so you know there is clear public health  advice  behind the position that we take and  that's that is what people should follow  because of course  education is very important as well  especially for people's long-term health  so you've got people watching this this  morning who know that in their area  where primary schools are opening  this morning there is a similar  infection rate to that in tier four  london  well you've got to follow the public  health advice and that's what we  are doing in this air in this space  whilst also  of course part of that public health uh  balance is the fact that education is  incredibly important to the  long-term prospects again the teachers  there are teachers up and down the  country who don't believe you  there are head teachers up and down the  country who are in direct disagreement  with  the secretary of state for education  this morning  it's pretty chaotic at the very least  well i i think  that that's uh a challenge that we have  across the whole of this response this  pandemic is you do  sometimes have to move fast and we have  moved fast for instance  in in saying that london schools  shouldn't  come back based on that public health  advice  and i know sometimes we get criticized  for  for moving fast and changing things but  that's what you have to do in a panel  but can't we act in anticipation we know  that the data doesn't accurately reflect  the transmission that will have taken  place in domestic environments  over the christmas period we know that  the case numbers are only going to get  worse  we know that the rate of transmission is  only going to get worse  why are we waiting to go for a full  national lockdown  well we've acted very rapidly to put  areas into tier four  and obviously we keep that data under  review all the time  and so that's that's a critical part of  the the job that we've got  and we then act very fast so for  instance you know when we saw the new  variant  arrive in uh and we got the advice  on it's the fact that it's more  contagious it transmits so much more  quickly  we then acted within 24 hours  and to put that into to put in further  measures that needed to be taken so  absolutely we're prepared to act when  it's necessary  following and listening to that public  health advice mr hancock good to see you  a busy few weeks ahead we'll let you go  on with it thanks for joining us thank  you very much

